\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9\'85Hast thou not\u8239?wondered,\u8239?Adam, at my stay? \par
Thee I have\u8239?missed, and thought it long,\u8239?deprived \par
\par
...but there is an argument that the post-lapsarian Eve is being disingenuous here: immediately after eating the apple she canvasses the idea of keeping the knowledge gained from eating the fruit of the forbidden tree to herself: \par
\'85shall I to him make knownAs yet my change, and give him to partakeFull happiness with me, or rather not,But keep the odds of knowledge in my power Without copartner?\par
(Paradise Lost, IX.817-821) \par
\par
Thy presence,\u8239?agony\u8239?of love\u8239?till now \par
Not felt, nor shall be twice, for never more \par
Mean I to\u8239?try, what\u8239?rash\u8239?untried\u8239?I sought,\u8239?\tab\tab\tab\tab 860  \par
The pain of absence from thy sight. But strange \par
Hath\u8239?been\u8239?the cause, and wonderful to\u8239?hear:\par
\par
We should note that Eve is deliberately over-praising the apple just as Satan did: this can be read as a symptom of her insecurity - she has, after all, gone against God's Law in eating the apple. The resemblance to Satan's hyperbole is deliberate - she is now "Satan's creature". \par
 \par
This tree is not as we are told, a tree \par
Of\u8239?danger\u8239?tasted, nor to evil unknown \par
Opening\u8239?the way, but of divine effect\u8239?\tab\tab\tab\tab 865 \par
To open eyes, and make them gods who taste; \par
\par
Note the sense of "mission creep" in Eve's words here: she began by wanting to be Adam's equal and now she wants to be God's equal!\par
\par
And hath\u8239?been\u8239?tasted\u8239?such: the serpent wise, \par
Or not\u8239?restrained\u8239?as\u8239?we, or not obeying, \par
Hath\u8239?eaten\u8239?of the fruit, and is become, \par
Not dead, as we are\u8239?threatened, but thenceforth\u8239?\tab\tab\tab 870 \par
Endued\u8239?with human voice and human sense, \par
Reasoning to admiration, and with\u8239?me \par
Persuasively\u8239?hath so\u8239?prevailed, that I \par
Have also tasted, and have also found \par
The\u8239?effects to correspond, opener mine eyes\u8239?\tab\tab\tab 875 \par
Dim\u8239?erst, dilated spirits, ampler heart, \par
And growing up to Godhead; which for thee \par
\par
The phrase "growing up to Godhead" gives the reader a strong sense of Eve's "Messiah complex". \par
\par
Chiefly I sought, without thee can despise. \par
For bliss, as thou hast part, to me is bliss, \par
\par
"Bliss" might seem hyperbolic - and is associated with human sexuality - but this is not unprecedented. Earlier in the poem, the apple was referred to in weirdly sexualised terms:\par
Meanwhile the hour of noon drew on, and wakedAn eager appetite, raised by the smellSo savoury of that fruit, which with desire,Inclinable now grown to touch or taste,Solicited her longing eye\'85\par
(PL: IX.740-744). \par
\par
Tedious,\u8239?unshared\u8239?with thee, and odious soon.\u8239?\tab\tab\tab 880 \par
Thou therefore also taste, that equal lot \par
May\u8239?join\u8239?us, equal joy, as equal love; \par
\par
The repetition of the word "equal" is not accidental here. Milton is keen to remind us that Eve's motivation for eating the apple in the first place was to gain equality in her relationship with Adam: we now suspect that she is keen to persuade Adam to eat the apple so that the two are "equal" in terms of their culpability.   \par
\par
Least\u8239?thou not tasting, different degree \par
Disjoin\u8239?us, and I then too late renounce \par
Deity\u8239?for thee, when fate will not permit.\u8239?\tab\tab\tab\tab 885 \par
Thus\u8239?Eve\u8239?with\u8239?countenance\u8239?blithe her\u8239?story\u8239?told; \par
But in her cheek distemper flushing\u8239?glowed. \par
\par
Eve is betrayed by her body here: while her "countenance [is] blithe", this is actually superficial; "in her cheek distemper glowed" (i.e. she is both angry and embarrassed at her behaviour). \par
\par
On\u8239?the\u8239?other side,\u8239?Adam, soon as he heard \par
The fatal trespass\u8239?done\u8239?by\u8239?Eve,\u8239?amazed, \par
Astonied\u8239?stood and blank, while horror chill\u8239?\tab\tab\tab 890 \par
Ran through his veins, and all his\u8239?joints\u8239?relaxed; \par
From his slack hand the garland\u8239?wreathed\u8239?for\u8239?Eve \par
Down\u8239?dropped, and all the faded roses shed\'85 \par
\par
A perfect example of the Pathetic Fallacy: Nature, again, "fe[els] the wound" (PL: IX.782) and the reader is given the sense of the enormity of The Fall.    \par
The dropping of the garland is symbolic of the sudden decline in their relationship - Eve has both literally and metaphorically "moved away" from Adam.\par
\par
(Paradise Lost, IX.856-893) \par
\par
Links to The Duchess of Malfi\'85 \par
\par
    Both Eve and Julia are examples of \ldblquote credulous women\rdblquote  (and their credulity is an \ldblquote essential\rdblquote  feature of their nature) whose credulity leads directly to their destruction (e.g. the poisoned Bible scene in Act 5, Scene 2). \par
\par
